## 4. The investigation — how the surprise unfolded

This section is the real story: shrinkage initially *hurt*, and fixing it taught
the method's true scope.

### 4.1 The metric — correlation (worked)

We test: does last season's rate predict next season's actual rate? For each
player we pair **A** = last-season rate (raw or shrunk) with **B** = next-season
actual rate, and correlate A against B across all players.

$$r = \frac{\sum(x_i-\bar x)(y_i-\bar y)}{\sqrt{\sum(x_i-\bar x)^2}\sqrt{\sum(y_i-\bar y)^2}}$$

Correlation runs −1 to +1: **+1** = last season perfectly predicts next; **0** =
tells you nothing. Higher = better predictor. Each prediction uses **one** prior
season, at **season level** (a player's whole-season rate, not per-gameweek —
gameweeks are far too noisy). B (the truth) is identical across raw/shrunk tests;
only A changes.

### 4.2 The surprise — shrinkage hurt everywhere

First attempt (npxG/90, single pooled noise):

| position | raw | shrunk |
|---|---|---|
| FWD | 0.675 | 0.646 |
| MID | 0.691 | 0.675 |
| DEF | 0.482 | 0.368 |

Shrinkage made prediction **worse** on every position — the opposite of its
purpose. Diagnosis: k was too big, because the **noise was overestimated** — we
reused the goals/Poisson formula on npxG (§3.2).

### 4.3 Correct noise, but defenders exploded

Plugging in the measured noise (0.0053) fixed FWD/MID (0.675→0.674, 0.691→0.684)
but **broke defenders**: k ≈ 128,000,000, correlation −0.089.

Cause: k = noise/signal explodes when signal ≈ 0. Defenders barely shoot, so their
*total* spread in npxG/90 is tiny; subtracting a noise value measured across
*everyone* (dominated by forwards) wiped out their entire spread → signal ≈ 0 → k
explodes → every defender shrunk to the same number → zero variance → garbage
correlation.

**Root cause: one pooled noise value for positions on totally different scales.**

### 4.4 Per-position noise — fixed defenders, but a new puzzle

Measuring split-half noise *within each position*:

```
FWD noise = 0.0195   (shoot most, biggest swings)
MID noise = 0.0059
DEF noise = 0.0016   (barely shoot — 12× less than forwards)
```

One number could never fit all three. With per-position noise:

| position | raw | shrunk | k |
|---|---|---|---|
| FWD | 0.675 | 0.605 | ≈47 |
| MID | 0.691 | 0.681 | ≈13 |
| DEF | 0.482 | 0.520 | ≈18 |

Defenders now *improve* (0.48→0.52). But a **puzzle**: forwards — the position we
predict *best* (0.675) — got the *heaviest* shrinkage (k≈47) and it hurt them.

### 4.5 Resolving the puzzle — two kinds of noise

The split-half measures **within-season** bounce (odd vs even gameweeks). Forwards
bounce a lot week-to-week (shot quality swings), so within-season noise is big → k
big → over-shrink.

But that bounce **washes out over a full season**. What shrinkage-for-prediction
needs is **cross-season** noise — whether the season *average* reflects durable
skill. The proof it does: forwards' raw rate predicts next season at 0.675, the
best of any position — a number that good *cannot* come from an unreliable
estimate.

We conflated within-season noise (large for forwards) with cross-season noise
(small for forwards). Two fixes, both tested:

**Option 1 — measure cross-season noise directly** (season-to-season bounce via
stable Understat id):

```
          cross-season   within-season
FWD          0.0092         0.0195      (half — bounce washes out)
MID          0.0045         0.0059
DEF          0.0016         0.0016      (same — defenders don't average out)
```

**Option 2 — skip the noise formula entirely; gridsearch k directly against
next-season correlation** (optimize the thing we actually care about):

| position | best k | reading |
|---|---|---|
| FWD | ≈2 | barely shrink |
| MID | ≈2 | barely shrink |
| DEF | ≈40 | shrink hard |

Both agree, and it is the **opposite** of the within-season formula for forwards.
The gridsearch curves are decisive: forwards' correlation *falls* as k rises
(k=2→0.676, k=80→0.542), while defenders' *rises* (k=0→0.482, k=40→0.522).

### 4.6 Robustness — the tuned k values were mostly false precision

The k values above (FWD/MID≈2, DEF≈40) came from just **2 season-pairs**. Before
trusting them, three checks — and they materially revised the conclusion.

**Check 1 — more data (8 season-pairs, Understat back to 2016, n≈500–970 vs
114–230).** The *direction* held (FWD/MID low-shrink, DEF moderate), but the
extremes regressed hard toward the middle: DEF npxG's "best k" moved 40→20, xA
MID's 5→10. **A best k that drifts with more data is partly noise**, not stable
signal. The correlation curves are flat-topped, so the argmax is dominated by
estimation error.

**Check 2 — does a single round k=10 match the tuned k?** Across all six
stat×position cells, universal k=10 landed within **0.01** of the tuned best in
every case (largest gap 0.009, most ≤0.003). The elaborate per-position-per-stat
tuning buys essentially nothing.

**Check 3 — the honest test: pick k on EARLY seasons, evaluate on LATE seasons.**
This is the only test that can't leak (the earlier gridsearch chose k with
knowledge of the same correlations it reported). Picking k on 2016–2020 and
scoring on 2020–2024:

| stat/pos | early-chosen k on late | universal k=10 on late |
|---|---|---|
| npxG FWD | 0.657 | 0.651 |
| npxG MID | 0.708 | 0.704 |
| npxG DEF | 0.461 | 0.461 |
| xA FWD | 0.523 | 0.521 |
| xA MID | 0.680 | **0.682** |
| xA DEF | 0.733 | **0.736** |

The honestly-chosen k beats k=10 by at most 0.006, and for xA MID/DEF **k=10
actually wins** — the tuning didn't even reliably pick the better value. This is
the clean proof: **per-position/per-stat k tuning provides no reliable
out-of-sample advantage.** Chasing it would have been overfitting to noise.

**The one stable exception: FWD npxG.** Across *every* test (2-season, 8-season,
early/late), forwards' npxG consistently preferred low k, and the curve always
declined monotonically as k rose — no drift, no wobble. This is mechanistically
sensible: forwards take the most shots, so their season npxG is the least-noisy
attacking rate we have, and deserves the least shrinkage. This exception is a real
*story*, not a fitted number, so it survives.

**Final rule (this supersedes §4.5's tuned values):**
- **FWD npxG: k = 2** (barely shrink — most reliable rate)
- **Everything else (npxG MID/DEF, all xA): k = 10**
- **Prior:** per-position, per-season average (unchanged; only k is simplified)

