## 7. Negative result — position × price-tier priors do not help

The master plan (§3.1) suggests shrinking toward **position × price-tier** priors
rather than a single position prior (a £12m forward toward the elite-forward
average, a £4.5m forward toward the budget average). Tested by attaching FPL price
(name-matched, ~78% coverage) and tiering within each position-season:

| position | position-only prior | position × tier prior |
|---|---|---|
| FWD | 0.645 | 0.643 |
| MID | 0.702 | 0.704 |
| DEF | 0.564 | 0.551 |

**No improvement anywhere** (differences within noise). Reason: a player's own npxG
**already encodes his quality** — expensive forwards *have* high npxG. Price and
npxG are strongly correlated, so once shrinking uses the player's own npxG, price
adds nothing. Price would help if the rate were uninformative; npxG is
informative, so price is redundant *for this purpose*.

**Where price still earns its place:** the minutes/nailedness model (we already use
it there, and it helps). Price predicts *whether a player plays* — which npxG
cannot. So price is valuable for minutes, just not for shrinking attacking rates.

(Caveat: Understat's multi-letter position labels — "D F M", "F M S" — made the
position filter loose, adding some noise. But a real effect this flat would not be
rescued by cleaner labels.)

